You can provide a custom comparator, such as `std::greater<int>()` or a lambda expression:
```cpp
std::vector<int> my_vec = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9};
std::sort(my_vec.begin(), my_vec.end(), std::greater<int>());
// Or with a lambda:
// std::sort(my_vec.begin(), my_vec.end(), [](int a, int b){ return a > b; });
```

```cpp
std::vector<int> input_vec = {1, 2, 3, 4};
std::vector<int> squared_vec;
squared_vec.reserve(input_vec.size()); // Optional: pre-allocate memory

std::transform(input_vec.begin(), input_vec.end(),
               std::back_inserter(squared_vec),
               [](int x){ return x * x; });
// squared_vec will be {1, 4, 9, 16}
```

The `std::accumulate` algorithm can be used for this:
```cpp
#include <numeric> // For std::accumulate
#include <list>

std::list<double> prices = {10.5, 20.0, 5.25};
double total_sum = std::accumulate(prices.begin(), prices.end(), 0.0);
// total_sum will be 35.75
```

```cpp
#include <algorithm> // For std::all_of
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> nums1 = {2, 4, 6, 8};
std::vector<int> nums2 = {2, 3, 6, 8};

bool all_even1 = std::all_of(nums1.begin(), nums1.end(), [](int n){ return n % 2 == 0; });
// all_even1 will be true

bool all_even2 = std::all_of(nums2.begin(), nums2.end(), [](int n){ return n % 2 == 0; });
// all_even2 will be false
```

You would prefer `std::find_if` when the search condition is more complex than a simple equality check. `std::find` searches for an exact value, while `std::find_if` searches for an element satisfying a unary predicate.

Example: To find the first odd number in a vector:
```cpp
std::vector<int> data = {2, 4, 7, 8, 9};
auto it = std::find_if(data.begin(), data.end(), [](int n){ return n % 2 != 0; });
// it will point to 7
```
